# Host crontab fragment for rpi-homeserver — generic jobs only.
#
# This file is NOT installed on its own. `scripts/deploy/install-crontab.sh` merges it with
# rpi-services/scripts/crontab (optional, skipped if that repo is not present) and installs the
# result, the same way Caddy imports config/caddy/services/. Personal jobs belong in the other
# repo's fragment, never here.
#
# scripts/deploy/apply.sh runs the installer on every deploy, so committing a change here applies it.
# Do NOT edit the live crontab with `crontab -e`: it gets overwritten on the next deploy.
# Show current:  crontab -l

# Auto-deploy: pull both repos and apply. The GitHub webhook (services/deploy-webhook)
# deploys on push, so this is now the every-30-min self-heal net: it restarts anything that
# died and covers pushes that arrived while the Pi or the tunnel was down.
*/30 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/deploy/apply.sh >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/apply.log 2>&1

# zram cost -> node-exporter textfile collector, every minute. node_exporter cannot tell swap on a
# disk from swap in compressed RAM, so its SWAP gauge sits red at 87% while nothing is wrong. This
# exports the number it is missing: the real RAM those pages occupy. One sysfs read.
* * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/metrics/zram.sh

# Largest files/dirs on the data disk -> node-exporter textfile collector, hourly.
# It was every 6h on the assumption that walking the disk is expensive; measured at 0.64s, so the
# only thing 6h bought was a panel showing files that had already been deleted.
0 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/metrics/disk-usage.sh >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/disk-usage-metrics.log 2>&1

# Radarr: search for anything missing or below its quality cutoff, nightly at 05:00.
# RSS already catches new releases within 30 min; this is for releases that scrolled out of the
# feed, which is why it needs an actual search. Add `sonarr` to cover series too.
#
# 05:00 and not 02:00, which is where it used to be: Plex runs its own nightly maintenance from
# 02:00 to 05:00 (its default window, and Preferences.xml does not override it). Measured on
# 2026-08-09, that costs 0.4 cores and 52 MB/s of *reads* with almost no writes. Downloads are the
# opposite shape, sustained writes, and both land on the same two USB platters. Starting the search
# as Plex finishes keeps the head doing one job at a time instead of seeking between two.
0 5 * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/ops/cutoff-search.sh >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/cutoff-search.log 2>&1

# Daily appdata backup at 04:00
0 4 * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/ops/backup.sh >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/backup.log 2>&1

# Dead man's switch: heartbeat to an external check every minute, so someone outside notices
# within a few minutes if the Pi goes dark. Costs ~0.2s of CPU per run (0.3% of one core) and no
# disk writes. No-op unless HEALTHCHECK_URL is set in .env. See docs/alerting.md.
* * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/ops/heartbeat.sh

# Seed-aware torrent cleanup: PARKED on 2026-08-24, deliberately left here rather than deleted.
# qbit-manage owns deletion now (config/qbit-manage/config.yml, share_limits with cleanup), and two
# deleters with different rules is the one arrangement worse than either alone. Uncomment this line
# and pause the qbit-manage groups to go back; see PENDING.md for what has to hold before the script
# and its two alert rules are deleted for good.
#20 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/trackers/seed-cleanup.py >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/seed-cleanup.log 2>&1

# Media stack "which one" metrics -> Pushgateway (quality upgrades, torrents by name, indexer
# status and usage) for the Media Pipeline dashboard. Read-only. See scripts/metrics/media.py.
*/5 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/metrics/media.py >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/media-metrics.log 2>&1

# Oracle's Always Free shapes in Madrid are permanently full, and a free tenancy cannot query
# capacity in advance, so the only way in is to keep asking. Every two minutes, one launch call per
# run: the ARM shape every time because it is the one worth having, the micro one run in five. The
# limit that matters is Oracle's own 429 on the attempts themselves, not this interval, so the
# script backs off (10 min, doubling to an hour) whenever it trips it and resets when a call goes
# through. Exits immediately once the instance exists, so this is safe to leave in forever.
# See scripts/ops/oci-hunt.py.
*/2 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/ops/oci-hunt.py >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/oci-hunt.log 2>&1

# What the tracker itself says about the account, every half hour: ratio, uploaded, downloaded, and
# the number that decides everything, the headroom of paid downloads left before the site disables
# the account. One page fetch per run, reusing a stored session cookie, because this is a tracker
# that bans for hammering. See scripts/trackers/stats.py.
*/30 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/trackers/stats.py >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/tracker-stats.log 2>&1

# Acts on that reading five minutes later: Radarr freeleech-only or not, the Sonarr indexer on or
# off (it has no freeleech flag, so the indexer is the switch), and the autobrr grab rate, which is
# a disk budget because a grab cannot be deleted until its hit & run window closes. Writes nothing
# when the values already match, refuses to act on a reading over 3 h old, and pauses the grabber
# below the free-space floor. See scripts/trackers/control.py; DRY_RUN=1 to see what it would change.
5,35 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/trackers/control.py >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/tracker-control.log 2>&1

# Clears Prowlarr's failure backoff on an indexer whose site is answering again, four times an hour.
# Prowlarr backs a failed indexer off for up to 24 h, and prowlarr_indexer_up reads that same
# disabledTill, so an hour of the ISP's match-night blocking used to cost a full day of the indexer
# being unusable and the alert firing over a site that works. Probes the site first and only then
# asks Prowlarr to test, so a tracker that is really down gets one HTTPS request per run and never a
# search. See scripts/ops/indexer-retry.py and docs/alerting.md.
8,23,38,53 * * * * /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/scripts/ops/indexer-retry.py >> /home/raspi/rpi-homeserver/indexer-retry.log 2>&1
